+# 06: Excel row numbers, blank rows, and short or long rows
+
+**What to build:** The row numbers in the Import report always match what Sales sees in Excel, however messy the sheet: blank rows in between, cells with line breaks, empty lines before the header. Blank rows are skipped without noise. Rows with too few cells still import where possible. Rows with too many cells are imported with a Row warning that the extras were dropped. See "Row numbering" and "Blank, short and long rows" in `.scratch/csv-import/spec.md`.
+
+**Blocked by:** 03 (Reject invalid rows with row errors)
+
+**Status:** resolved
+
+- [ ] Every CSV record counts toward `RowIssue.row`, blank rows included. A contact after two blank lines gets the number Excel shows.
+- [ ] A quoted cell containing line breaks counts as one row, so rows after it keep their Excel numbers.
+- [ ] Blank lines before the header are allowed. The header is the first non-blank row, and numbering keeps counting from the top of the file (e.g. the header may be row 3).
+- [ ] A row whose cells are all blank, including a row of only commas, is skipped silently: it's not in the report and changes nothing.
+- [ ] A row with fewer cells than the header treats the missing cells as blank (e.g. a missing email gives the usual "is required" error on `email`).
+- [ ] A row with more cells than the header is processed normally, ignoring the extras, and gets a Row warning with `column=None` saying how many extra cells were ignored.
+
+## Comments
+
+Implemented on integration branch `csv-import` (merged in 248e13f; code-review fixes in 85880f7). Tests: `tests/test_import*.py`.
