+# 08: Bytes input and rejecting unreadable files
+
+**What to build:** Callers can pass the file exactly as read from disk. UTF-8 bytes, including Excel's "CSV UTF-8" with its BOM, import cleanly. Files contactbook can't read reliably are rejected as a whole with a message telling Sales how to fix them, and nothing is imported: non-UTF-8 encodings, semicolon-separated files and structurally broken CSV. See "Decoding and CSV dialect" in `.scratch/csv-import/spec.md`.
+
+**Blocked by:** 01 (Import a clean file into an empty contactbook)
+
+**Status:** resolved
+
+- [ ] `bytes` input is decoded as strict UTF-8 and behaves exactly like the equivalent `str` input.
+- [ ] A leading BOM is stripped from both `bytes` and `str` input, so the first column still matches `name`.
+- [ ] Bytes that aren't valid UTF-8 (e.g. a cp1252-encoded accented name) raise `ImportFileError` with a hint to re-save as "CSV UTF-8" in Excel. No other encoding is tried.
+- [ ] A header that is a single cell containing `;` (e.g. `name;email;phone;tags`) raises `ImportFileError` saying the file looks semicolon-separated.
+- [ ] A CSV the parser can't read (e.g. an unclosed quote) raises `ImportFileError`.
+- [ ] In every case above, the repository is unchanged.
+
+## Comments
+
+Implemented on integration branch `csv-import` (merged in 0937cb1; code-review fixes in 85880f7). Tests: `tests/test_import*.py`.
