+++ b/.scratch/csv-import/spec.md
@@ -0,0 +1,181 @@
+# CSV Import
+
+Status: resolved
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Sales keeps its leads in spreadsheets. Getting them into contactbook today means adding Contacts one at a time, which is slow and error-prone for sheets with hundreds of rows. When something is wrong with a row there is no way to find out which row, or why, short of trying each one by hand. Sales needs to hand contactbook a whole CSV file and get back a clear account of what happened.
+
+## Solution
+
+An **Import** loads a whole CSV file in one action. The file has the same columns as contactbook's **Export**: name, email, phone and tags. Rows with a new email create a Contact. Rows with an email already in contactbook update that Contact by merging the row into it. Rows that can't be imported are skipped. Every Import returns an **Import report** that lists the emails created and updated, plus each **Row error** and **Row warning**. Each issue carries the spreadsheet row number Sales sees in Excel and the column it concerns. Files that can't be imported at all (wrong encoding, missing columns, more than 1000 contact rows, and so on) are rejected as a whole with a clear reason, and nothing is imported.
+
+A lead is simply a Contact. Import does not add any extra marker to the Contacts it creates.
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a sales rep, I want to load a whole CSV file of contacts in one action, so that I don't have to add them one by one.
+2. As a sales rep, I want to use a file in the same format as contactbook's Export, so that I can export, edit in a spreadsheet and re-import.
+3. As a sales rep, I want rows with a new email to become new Contacts, so that my leads end up in contactbook.
+4. As a sales rep, I want rows with an email that already exists to update that Contact, not fail, so that I can re-import an updated sheet.
+5. As a sales rep, I want emails matched regardless of case and surrounding spaces, so that `Ann@Example.com ` and `ann@example.com` are treated as the same Contact.
+6. As a sales rep, I want an updated Contact's name to take the name from my file, so that corrections to names come through.
+7. As a sales rep, I want a blank phone cell to leave an existing Contact's phone alone, so that a sheet where we just don't know the number doesn't wipe numbers we already have.
+8. As a sales rep, I want a filled-in phone cell to replace the existing phone, so that new numbers come through.
+9. As a sales rep, I want tags in my file added to an existing Contact's tags, so that an import never silently removes tags someone else added.
+10. As a sales rep, I want a report listing which emails were created, so that I know which leads are new.
+11. As a sales rep, I want a report listing which emails were updated, so that I know which existing Contacts my file changed.
+12. As a sales rep, I want rows that change nothing to be left out of the updated list, so that re-importing an Export shows an empty report rather than a wall of "updated".
+13. As a sales rep, I want each problem reported with the row number Excel shows me, so that I can jump straight to the offending row.
+14. As a sales rep, I want blank rows counted in row numbers, so that the numbers still match Excel when my sheet has gaps.
+15. As a sales rep, I want a cell with line breaks in it to count as one row, so that the row numbers still match Excel after a multi-line notes cell.
+16. As a sales rep, I want each problem to say which column it's in (name, email, phone or tags), so that I know which cell to fix.
+17. As a sales rep, I want every problem in a row reported at once, so that I don't fix one cell, re-import and discover the next.
+18. As a sales rep, I want the valid rows imported even when some rows are bad, so that one typo doesn't block the whole sheet.
+19. As a sales rep, I want a row with a missing or invalid email skipped and reported, so that no Contact is created without a way to identify it.
+20. As a sales rep, I want a row with a missing or invalid name skipped and reported, so that every Contact has a proper name.
+21. As a sales rep, I want a row with an invalid tag skipped and reported, so that bad tags don't pollute contactbook.
+22. As a sales rep, I want a skipped row to change nothing at all, not even part of an existing Contact, so that I can trust that "error" means "untouched".
+23. As a sales rep, I want a row with a bad phone number still imported, with the phone left out and a warning, so that a typo in a phone number doesn't cost me the lead.
+24. As a sales rep, I want a bad phone on an existing Contact to keep the phone we already had, so that a typo doesn't erase a good number.
+25. As a sales rep, I want a skipped row's bad phone reported as an error next to its other errors, so that the list of things to fix on that row is complete.
+26. As a sales rep, I want a blank name on an existing Contact reported as an error, not treated as "keep the old name", so that the rules for names are the same for new and existing Contacts.
+27. As a sales rep, I want a second row with the same email as an earlier row reported as a duplicate of that row, so that I can see and resolve the duplication in my sheet.
+28. As a sales rep, I want duplicate rows to be reported even if the first copy was itself skipped, so that which row "wins" never depends on errors elsewhere in the file.
+29. As a sales rep, I want multiple tags in one cell separated by semicolons, as in the Export, so that the format is the same both ways.
+30. As a sales rep, I want spaces around tags trimmed and empty pieces ignored (`vip; ;prospect;`), so that small typing slips don't cause errors.
+31. As a sales rep, I want repeated tags in a cell collapsed, so that `vip;VIP` is just one tag.
+32. As a sales rep, I want a comma inside a tags cell reported as an invalid tag, so that I learn the correct separator rather than getting a surprise.
+33. As a sales rep, I want completely blank rows (including rows of only commas) skipped silently, so that trailing empty rows in a spreadsheet don't clutter the report.
+34. As a sales rep, I want a row with fewer cells than the header to treat the missing cells as blank, so that hand-edited short rows still import where possible.
+35. As a sales rep, I want a row with more cells than the header imported with a warning that the extra cells were ignored, so that I know data was dropped.
+36. As a sales rep, I want column headers matched regardless of case, surrounding spaces and order, so that `Email, Name` works as well as `name,email`.
+37. As a sales rep, I want extra columns such as `company` or `notes` ignored with a warning, so that I can import my sheet as-is without deleting columns first.
+38. As a sales rep, I want the phone and tags columns to be optional, so that a sheet with only names and emails imports fine.
+39. As a sales rep, I want a file from the address-book tool with `first_name` and `last_name` columns accepted, with the name being the two joined by one space, so that I don't have to merge columns by hand.
+40. As a sales rep, I want a row with only a first name or only a last name to get just that part as its name, so that single-name contacts import cleanly.
+41. As a sales rep, I want problems with first or last name reported under the name column, so that the report reads the same whichever format I used.
+42. As a sales rep, I want a file missing the name or email column rejected with a clear message, so that I don't get an error on every single row.
+43. As a sales rep, I want a file with the same column twice, or with both `name` and `first_name`/`last_name`, rejected, so that contactbook never has to guess which column I meant.
+44. As a sales rep, I want a file saved by Excel as "CSV UTF-8" (with a BOM) to import cleanly, so that the most common Excel export just works.
+45. As a sales rep, I want a file that isn't UTF-8 rejected with instructions to re-save it as "CSV UTF-8", so that names with accents never import garbled.
+46. As a sales rep, I want a semicolon-separated file rejected with a message saying so, so that I know to re-save it with commas.
+47. As a sales rep, I want blank lines before the header tolerated, so that a stray empty first line doesn't break the import.
+48. As a sales rep, I want an empty file rejected with "file is empty", so that I know I picked the wrong file.
+49. As a sales rep, I want a file with only a header to be accepted with an empty report, so that an empty template is not treated as an error.
+50. As a sales rep, I want a file with more than 1000 contact rows rejected as a whole, so that I split it into smaller files rather than end up with a half-loaded sheet.
+51. As a sales rep, I want blank rows not to count toward the 1000-row limit, so that trailing empty rows don't push a valid file over it.
+52. As a sales rep, I want a rejected file to change nothing in contactbook, so that I can fix the file and simply try again.
+53. As a developer of the HTTP layer or a CLI, I want a single function that takes the repository and the file content (raw bytes or text), so that I can wire up import without knowing CSV details.
+54. As a developer of the HTTP layer or a CLI, I want whole-file failures raised as an error that derives from the common contactbook error, so that my existing "catch ContactError and show the message" handling covers import too.
+55. As a developer of the HTTP layer or a CLI, I want row-level problems returned in the report rather than raised, so that I can show all of them to the user at once.
+56. As a developer, I want imported values to go through the same validators as hand-entered Contacts, so that lookups by email keep working and stored data stays consistent.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+### Modules
+
+- **New importer module** in the contactbook package holds the whole feature: `import_contacts`, `ImportReport` and `RowIssue`.
+- **Errors module** gains `ImportFileError(ContactError)`, raised when the file as a whole can't be imported. It carries a human-readable message suitable for showing to the user. Export it from the package root next to the other domain errors.
+- The README layout section gains a line for the importer module.
+- No changes to `Contact`, the validators, the repository or `ContactBook`. Import builds and stores Contacts only through the existing validators/`Contact.create`-style normalisation and the repository's `add` / `update` / `find`.
+
+### Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+- `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`. `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` dataclass: `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised emails, in file order), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]` (in row order).
+- `RowIssue` dataclass: `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- Passing something that is neither `str` nor `bytes` is a programming error (`TypeError`), not an `ImportFileError`, in line with the errors module's convention.
+
+### All-or-nothing at file level, partial at row level
+
+- The whole file is decoded, parsed and every row checked **before the repository is touched**. Raising `ImportFileError` therefore always means the repository is unchanged.
+- After that, every row without a Row error is applied. Rows with a Row error are not applied at all, not even partly to an existing Contact.
+
+### Decoding and CSV dialect (otherwise `ImportFileError`)
+
+- `bytes` are decoded as strict UTF-8. A leading BOM is stripped from both `bytes` and `str` input. Bytes that aren't valid UTF-8 (e.g. Excel's cp1252 "CSV") raise `ImportFileError` with a hint to re-save as "CSV UTF-8" in Excel. No guessing of other encodings.
+- Comma delimiter only, standard CSV quoting. If the header is a single cell that contains `;` (e.g. `name;email;phone;tags`), raise `ImportFileError` saying the file looks semicolon-separated.
+- A structurally broken CSV that the parser rejects raises `ImportFileError`.
+- An empty file, or one containing only whitespace / blank rows, raises `ImportFileError("file is empty")`-style.
+
+### Row numbering
+
+- `RowIssue.row` is the row number Excel shows: every CSV record counts, blank rows included, and a quoted cell with embedded line breaks counts as one row. The first row of the file is row 1.
+- The header is the first non-blank row. Leading blank rows before it still count, so the header may be row 3 and the first data row row 4.
+- Issues about the header itself (ignored unknown columns) use the header's row number.
+
+### Header rules (otherwise `ImportFileError`)
+
+- Column names are matched case-insensitively with surrounding whitespace trimmed, in any order.
+- Recognised columns: `name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`, plus the pair `first_name` + `last_name` as an alternative to `name`.
+- `email` is required. A name source is required: either `name`, or both `first_name` and `last_name`. Having only one of `first_name`/`last_name` (and no `name`) counts as a missing name column.
+- `phone` and `tags` are optional. A missing column is treated as blank in every row.
+- A recognised column appearing twice, or `name` together with `first_name`/`last_name`, raises `ImportFileError`.
+- Unknown columns are ignored, with one Row warning each: `column=None` and a message naming the header as written (e.g. `ignored unknown column "Company"`).
+
+### Row limit
+
+- At most **1000 contact rows** per file. A contact row is any non-blank data row after the header, **including rows that would be rejected**. Blank rows don't count.
+- 1000 contact rows are accepted. 1001 or more raise `ImportFileError` and nothing is imported.
+- A header with no data rows is valid and returns an empty `ImportReport`.
+
+### Blank, short and long rows
+
+- A row whose cells are all blank (including a row of only commas) is skipped silently. It still counts toward row numbering but not toward the row limit, and it doesn't appear in the report.
+- A row with fewer cells than the header: missing cells are blank.
+- A row with more cells than the header: the extra cells are ignored and the row is otherwise processed normally, with a Row warning (`column=None`, message saying how many extra cells were ignored).
+
+### Field rules
+
+- `RowIssue.column` is always one of `name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`, or `None`. Problems with `first_name` / `last_name` are reported under `name`.
+- **email**: normalised with the existing email validator. Missing or invalid → Row error on `email`.
+- **name**: normalised with the existing name validator. With `first_name`/`last_name`, each part is trimmed, blank parts are dropped and the rest joined with one space. Both blank → "is required". Missing or invalid → Row error on `name`, **for new and existing Contacts alike** (a blank name never means "keep the stored name").
+- **tags**: the cell is split on `;` only. Each piece is trimmed and empty pieces are dropped. Each remaining piece goes through the existing tag validator, and repeats collapse after normalisation. Any invalid tag (including one containing a comma) → Row error on `tags`.
+- **phone**: blank means "no phone given". A non-blank value goes through the existing phone validator. An invalid phone is a **Row warning** on `phone` when the row is otherwise valid: the row is still applied, and the phone is treated as not given (none for a new Contact, stored phone kept for an existing one). If the row is rejected for another reason, the invalid phone is reported as a **Row error** on `phone` alongside the others, since rejected rows carry only errors.
+- Each invalid field produces exactly one RowIssue, whose message is the validator's message (without the field name). All invalid fields in a row are reported, not just the first.
+
+### Duplicate emails within the file
+
+- Rows are compared by normalised email. The first row with a given email is processed normally. Every later row with the same email is a Row error on `email` (message referring to the first row, e.g. "duplicate of row 5") and is not applied.
+- This holds even if the first row was itself rejected. Being a duplicate depends only on the file, not on which rows were applied.
+- Rows whose email is missing or invalid take no part in duplicate detection.
+
+### Applying a valid row
+
+- **New email**: create a Contact from the row's name, email, phone (if valid and given) and tags, and append its normalised email to `created`.
+- **Existing email (merge)**: name is overwritten with the row's name. A valid, non-blank phone replaces the stored phone, otherwise the stored phone is kept. The row's tags are added to the stored tags (never removed). Store the result with the repository's update.
+- If the merged Contact equals the stored one, the row is a no-op: it appears in **neither** `created` nor `updated` and produces no warning. Otherwise append the normalised email to `updated`. As a result, re-importing an Export yields an empty report.
+- A row with only warnings is still listed in `created` / `updated` as appropriate.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam:** every test goes through `import_contacts(repo, data)` with a real `InMemoryContactRepository`. Tests check what can be observed from outside: the returned `ImportReport` (lists and exact `RowIssue`s), the Contacts in the repository afterwards, and `ImportFileError` with the repository unchanged. Internal helpers (decoding, header reading, row checking) are not tested directly and may be reshaped freely.
+- Good tests describe behaviour Sales or a caller would recognise ("a bad phone still creates the contact, with a warning on row 3"), not how the importer is built. Assert on exact row numbers and columns. For messages, assert the parts that matter (e.g. the referenced row in a duplicate error) and don't hard-code every word.
+- Inputs are small CSVs written inline in the test, as `str` and as `bytes`. Include real edge-case bytes: UTF-8 with BOM, cp1252-encoded accented names, quoted multi-line cells, rows of only commas, leading blank lines.
+- Cover at least: create, merge update (name / phone kept / phone replaced / tags added), no-op re-import, Export→Import round trip, each Row error and Row warning, row numbering with blank rows and multi-line cells, header variants (order/case/unknown/duplicate/missing/first+last), duplicate emails (including after a rejected first copy), skipped rows leaving existing Contacts untouched, every `ImportFileError` cause leaving the repository untouched, the 1000/1001 boundary with blank rows not counting, and header-only files.
+- Prior art: `tests/test_export.py` and `tests/test_service.py`. These are plain pytest functions, one behaviour per test, with small literal inputs and exact assertions. The round-trip test can use the existing `export_csv` to produce its input.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Encodings other than UTF-8, and guessing the encoding or delimiter.
+- Semicolon- or tab-separated files.
+- Removing tags or clearing phones through import (import only adds or overwrites).
+- Deleting Contacts that are missing from the file.
+- A dry-run / preview mode, or an option to abort on the first row error.
+- Files over 1000 contact rows (split them), streaming, and progress reporting.
+- Columns beyond name, email, phone, tags and first_name/last_name. Others are ignored.
+- Any notion of "lead" separate from Contact, or automatically tagging imported Contacts.
+- HTTP upload handling, CLI commands and persistence adapters, which live in other packages.
+- Thread safety (the repository isn't thread-safe either).
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Domain vocabulary (Contact, Export, Import, Import report, Row error, Row warning) is defined in `GLOSSARY.md`. "Lead" is a synonym to avoid.
+- The "address-book export" with `first_name` / `last_name` is a file from an outside tool, not contactbook's own Export. It's supported only through the header rule above.
+- Treating an invalid phone as a warning, not an error, was a deliberate choice: losing a lead over a phone typo is worse than importing it without a number. Every other invalid field rejects the row.
+- No ADRs were recorded: none of these decisions is costly to reverse.
+
+## Comments
+
+All tickets 01–09 implemented on integration branch `csv-import` (tip 85880f7), code-reviewed and fixed. 91 tests pass.
